  "text": "Sunlight intensity appears to be the chief driver distinguishing open, sun‑exposed sites from shaded ones. Plants growing in shade do not react to a single cue; they integrate all surrounding conditions. Although the exact variables differed, the overall environmental setting at two separate locations produced comparable functional behavior in a typical coastal shrub community. The arid appearance of vegetation on sandy dunes is linked more to the nature of the soil than to the regional climate. Soil temperature below roughly 3–4 °C strongly restricts water loss through leaves, while warmer soils boost transpiration until the response levels off, a pattern that warrants further investigation. Leaf water loss was greatest in soils with moderate moisture, lower in fully saturated soils, and minimal in dry soils. Correspondingly, the measured osmotic potential consistently fell as soil moisture increased."
